            <ArticleTitle>Epithelial transport in The Journal of General Physiology.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>897-909</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1085/jgp.201711828</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Epithelia define the boundaries of the body and often transfer solutes and water from outside to inside (absorption) or from inside to outside (secretion). Those processes involve dual plasma membranes with different transport components that interact with each other. Understanding those functions has entailed breaking down the problem to analyze properties of individual membranes (apical vs. basolateral) and individual transport proteins. It also requires understanding of how those components interact and how they are regulated. This article outlines the modern history of this research as reflected by publications in The Journal of General Physiology.</AbstractText>
